#Objective
Configure a Linux firewall to :
•	control network access,
•	reduce the attack surface,
•	allow only necessary services, and
•	secure the server.
Using : UFW


#Context
In professional environments :
•	not all ports should be accessible,
•	only necessary services should be exposed,
•	unauthorized connections must be blocked.
A firewall is therefore an essential layer of security.


#Network Security Policy
| Rule             | Action   |
| ---------------- | -------- |
| Incoming Traffic | Deny     |
| Outgoing Traffic | Allow    |
| SSH              | Allow    |
| HTTP             | Optional |
| HTTPS            | Optional |


#Verify UFW installation
sudo ufw status
sudo apt install ufw -y
sudo systemctl status ufw --no-pager


#Reset Existing Rules
sudo ufw reset


#Configure Default Policies
sudo ufw default deny incoming
sudo ufw default allow outgoing
sudo ufw status verbose


#Allow SSH Access
sudo ufw allow ssh
sudo ufw allow 22/tcp
sudo ufw status numbered


#Enable Firewall
sudo ufw enable
sudo ufw status verbose


#Advanced Rules (Optional)
sudo ufw allow 80/tcp
sudo ufw allow 443/tcp
sudo ufw status numbered
sudo ss -tulnp
sudo ufw status verbose
sudo iptables -L

